<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juan Rulfo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1917–1986</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juan Rulfo was a Mexican writer and photographer whose small body of work had great influence on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> American literature. His story collection The Burning Plain (1953) and novel Pedro Páramo (1955), set among the dead of a ruined village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalisco">Jalisco</a>, portray rural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> after the Revolution. Gabriel García Márquez acknowledged Pedro Páramo as a major influence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juan-rulfo.jpg" alt="Juan Rulfo" width="320" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rulfo_por_Lyon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
